WEBVTT
Kind: captions

00:00:01.358 --> 00:00:03.705
Hello and welcome to 170A.

00:00:03.705 --> 00:00:08.805
I am here to show you how to set up
GitHub so that you can host some

00:00:08.805 --> 00:00:10.585
stuff on a repository.

00:00:10.585 --> 00:00:11.685
My name is Abram Stern.

00:00:11.685 --> 00:00:13.446
I'm your instructor this quarter.

00:00:13.446 --> 00:00:15.726
And I co designed this
class with Warren Sack.

00:00:15.726 --> 00:00:20.537
So you'll hear my voice in the code talk
throughs little bit later in the

00:00:20.537 --> 00:00:23.494
quarter, not so much later.

00:00:23.494 --> 00:00:30.599
And Warren, Professor Warren Sack on the
lectures, we collaborated on this and

00:00:30.599 --> 00:00:33.110
class benefited from having two cooks.

00:00:33.110 --> 00:00:38.681
So this window here is my GitHub account.

00:00:38.681 --> 00:00:43.083
And this video, this brief video
walkthrough assumes that

00:00:43.083 --> 00:00:45.036
you have already registered [for Github].

00:00:45.036 --> 00:00:49.190
There are some other services you can use
since the wrong window.

00:00:49.190 --> 00:00:51.327
Okay, we're over here.

00:00:51.327 --> 00:00:54.627
GitLab.com is another one that you could
use if you really don't

00:00:54.627 --> 00:00:55.967
want to use GitHub.

00:00:55.967 --> 00:00:58.819
And then there's Codeberg.org.

00:00:58.819 --> 00:01:00.779
Codeberg is based in the EU.

00:01:00.779 --> 00:01:05.342
They have various privacy settings and
they don't allow the use of AI in

00:01:05.342 --> 00:01:06.765
any of their projects.

00:01:06.765 --> 00:01:16.788
So all of these sites have their
own pages capability feature.

00:01:16.788 --> 00:01:20.510
But they're all a little bit different
and GitHub is the easiest to set up.

00:01:20.510 --> 00:01:26.328
So if you're just getting started and you
don't have any major problem using

00:01:26.328 --> 00:01:28.728
GitHub, I recommend doing that.

00:01:28.728 --> 00:01:30.448
And if you want to use one of these other
ones, maybe you're a

00:01:30.448 --> 00:01:33.123
little more advanced.

00:01:33.123 --> 00:01:34.383
That's fine too.

00:01:34.383 --> 00:01:37.863
And if you get stuck trying to get things
working, feel free to reach out.

00:01:37.863 --> 00:01:41.583
But otherwise, I'm going to be showing
how to use GitHub for this.

00:01:41.583 --> 00:01:43.703
So your page may look
a little bit different.

00:01:43.703 --> 00:01:46.303
For instance, you may
not have 67 repositories.

00:01:46.303 --> 00:01:50.302
What have I been doing with my life?

00:01:50.302 --> 00:01:51.942
And your background might be white.

00:01:51.942 --> 00:01:54.265
I am dark mode all the time.

00:01:54.265 --> 00:01:56.945
So that's why my page looks like this.

00:01:56.945 --> 00:01:58.325
I'm going to make a new repository.

00:01:58.325 --> 00:02:00.945
And I do that here from this plus sign up
here, this plus menu

00:02:00.945 --> 00:02:02.658
that makes new things.

00:02:02.658 --> 00:02:05.558
And I'm going to make a repository to
store all of the assets

00:02:05.558 --> 00:02:10.736
for my 170A project.

00:02:10.736 --> 00:02:14.696
And if you want to be really organized,
you could make a repository for each of

00:02:14.696 --> 00:02:18.576
your projects, even though you're going
to be basically hosting them on CodePen

00:02:18.576 --> 00:02:20.216
if you also wanted to have them on here.

00:02:20.216 --> 00:02:25.187
So you had a version that just ran full
screen without CodePen's

00:02:25.187 --> 00:02:28.340
triple window stuff.

00:02:28.340 --> 00:02:29.680
You can do that.

00:02:29.680 --> 00:02:31.552
And it's not so hard.

00:02:31.552 --> 00:02:34.773
If you want to put everything in one
repository, that's OK, too.

00:02:34.773 --> 00:02:43.914
You can upload images, sounds, HTML
files, JavaScript, CSS, you name it.

00:02:43.914 --> 00:02:45.954
The main thing to avoid is trying to
upload files that are

00:02:45.954 --> 00:02:47.454
more than 100 megabytes.

00:02:47.454 --> 00:02:50.725
And when we get into video later in the
quarter, that may be a

00:02:50.725 --> 00:02:51.485
wall you run into.

00:02:51.485 --> 00:02:53.725
And we can work on that then.

00:02:53.725 --> 00:03:01.358
So I'm just going to call
this FDM170A_Projects.

00:03:01.358 --> 00:03:03.798
And it will be underneath the owner name.

00:03:03.798 --> 00:03:05.910
So this is your account name here.

00:03:05.910 --> 00:03:07.130
And then this is this.

00:03:07.130 --> 00:03:12.511
So each person can have a repository name
that is like, the repository

00:03:12.511 --> 00:03:13.942
names can be identical.

00:03:13.942 --> 00:03:21.862
Since the address is beneath the
username, they don't collide.

00:03:21.862 --> 00:03:31.621
And this will be some files to support my
projects, images and such,

00:03:31.621 --> 00:03:33.821
and a smiley face.

00:03:33.821 --> 00:03:37.523
Because I grew up before emoji, so I
still type them by hand.

00:03:37.523 --> 00:03:40.303
Visibility public is important.

00:03:40.303 --> 00:03:43.783
If you don't want your projects to be
publicly available, that makes things

00:03:43.783 --> 00:03:45.023
quite a bit more complicated.

00:03:45.023 --> 00:03:46.243
We can work around that.

00:03:46.243 --> 00:03:48.163
It's really a hard line for you.

00:03:48.163 --> 00:03:50.384
But public should be default here.

00:03:50.384 --> 00:03:52.485
And this other stuff is just
fine as it is.

00:03:52.485 --> 00:03:57.018
We're going to create repository and it's
going to say like, hey, this is empty.

00:03:57.018 --> 00:03:59.504
Don't you want to do something?

00:03:59.504 --> 00:04:04.764
And for instance, setting up GitHub
Copilot, which is Microsoft's like AI

00:04:04.764 --> 00:04:08.568
companion, which I would
prefer you didn't use.

00:04:08.568 --> 00:04:12.128
You can add collaborators so that you can
share code and like deal with the

00:04:12.128 --> 00:04:17.893
versioning of a project as
it changes over time.

00:04:17.893 --> 00:04:22.336
Git is the underlying technology that all
three of these sites use.

00:04:22.336 --> 00:04:26.556
And then GitHub, GitLab, and Codeberg
each implement their own kind of

00:04:26.556 --> 00:04:32.088
architecture around Git that adds social
features like issues, like reporting

00:04:32.088 --> 00:04:36.059
bugs, pull requests, like somebody else
making a change and submitting

00:04:36.059 --> 00:04:37.179
it for review.

00:04:37.179 --> 00:04:42.551
Like that's social architecture on top of
the versioning architecture of Git.

00:04:42.551 --> 00:04:45.311
And you're going to see these like
command line things that you could type

00:04:45.311 --> 00:04:47.891
if you installed Git
and used your terminal.

00:04:47.891 --> 00:04:50.162
We're not going to worry
about that right now.

00:04:50.162 --> 00:04:52.004
We're just going to use the web page.

00:04:52.004 --> 00:04:56.004
So it's saying we can get started by
creating a new file and they have a web

00:04:56.004 --> 00:05:00.665
based text editor that you can do to
just edit files in your browser and save

00:05:00.665 --> 00:05:05.047
them in your repository.

00:05:05.485 --> 00:05:09.257
Oh, there's a dash in my, did
I really do that?

00:05:09.257 --> 00:05:10.569
I didn't mean to do that.

00:05:10.569 --> 00:05:13.239
Well, you can call yours
whatever you want.

00:05:13.239 --> 00:05:15.659
I unintentionally put
a hyphen after project.

00:05:15.659 --> 00:05:17.069
I don't know how I did that.

00:05:17.069 --> 00:05:23.948
Anyway, I'm going to upload an existing
file and it gives you this dialogue

00:05:23.948 --> 00:05:26.788
that's familiar from
other websites I imagine.

00:05:26.788 --> 00:05:32.128
And I'm going to drag this file of 
"rosie_a.png"

00:05:32.128 --> 00:05:38.911
Rosie is a dog that my partner and I take
care of and take for walks every day.

00:05:38.911 --> 00:05:42.748
She's a sweet girl.

00:05:42.748 --> 00:05:44.984
And now it's asking me to commit changes.

00:05:44.984 --> 00:05:47.924
And so part of Git is that
there are commit messages.

00:05:47.924 --> 00:05:51.044
Every time you make a change to the
repository, you're making a note

00:05:51.044 --> 00:05:52.364
about what you did.

00:05:52.364 --> 00:05:56.084
And this is like a trail of bread crumbs
that you can follow backwards in time

00:05:56.084 --> 00:05:57.915
over the growth of a project.

00:05:57.915 --> 00:06:01.825
If you make a ton of changes and commit
them all together, you don't have the

00:06:01.825 --> 00:06:06.337
granularity of change this file, change
this file change this file.

00:06:06.337 --> 00:06:15.082
In this case, I just uploaded Rosie.

00:06:15.082 --> 00:06:22.675
So all of this is so that we can host
images and media and use them in CodePen

00:06:22.675 --> 00:06:26.469
because they will host the HTML, the
CSS, all the text stuff.

00:06:26.469 --> 00:06:31.936
Text, not tech, but it's also
kind of tech, right?

00:06:31.936 --> 00:06:35.059
But they don't really have a file
upload thing for images.

00:06:35.059 --> 00:06:41.811
So previously, UCSC had their own web
server that everybody got an account on,

00:06:41.811 --> 00:06:44.194
students, staff, and faculty.

00:06:44.194 --> 00:06:48.661
And it had its quirks, but it was nice.

00:06:48.661 --> 00:06:52.281
But that hasn't existed for
a few years now.

00:06:52.281 --> 00:06:56.384
Then we were using Dropbox in a way that
Dropbox really wasn't

00:06:56.384 --> 00:06:58.424
meant for: hotlinking images.

00:06:58.424 --> 00:07:03.418
And you ended up with these URLs that are
like 17 miles long with lots of random

00:07:03.418 --> 00:07:04.898
looking numbers in it.

00:07:04.898 --> 00:07:11.188
I'm sorry, that's, I'll stop that.

00:07:11.329 --> 00:07:14.900
GitHub Pages is the lowest hanging fruit
to solve this problem.

00:07:14.900 --> 00:07:19.340
It gives us a way to host files on the
web as though on a webpage that we can

00:07:19.340 --> 00:07:21.344
then link from outside.

00:07:21.344 --> 00:07:26.365
So if I want to use this image of Rosie
in a project, I can host it on pages and

00:07:26.365 --> 00:07:29.637
I can host my whole project on here too.

00:07:29.637 --> 00:07:33.536
So how do we do that?

00:07:33.536 --> 00:07:38.742
Well, there's a menu up here
in our project, right?

00:07:38.742 --> 00:07:39.862
Code issues, pull requests.

00:07:39.862 --> 00:07:42.518
These are all for this repository.

00:07:42.518 --> 00:07:48.630
And because I've bumped the font size up
to make this video easier to read,

00:07:48.630 --> 00:07:50.620
settings was getting hidden
in the more menu.

00:07:50.620 --> 00:07:51.220
See?

00:07:51.220 --> 00:07:53.753
More over here, settings.

00:07:53.753 --> 00:07:55.993
But if it's wide then
we get settings here.

00:07:55.993 --> 00:07:58.407
So settings is where we want.

00:07:58.407 --> 00:08:00.907
And this is a lot of stuff that we don't
need to worry about.

00:08:00.907 --> 00:08:02.367
Some of it's pretty complicated.

00:08:02.367 --> 00:08:05.367
Actions and webhooks are like code that
we can write that will automatically

00:08:05.367 --> 00:08:09.108
happen every time the code changes, to
sort of like test things.

00:08:09.108 --> 00:08:11.072
And like we're not doing that.

00:08:11.072 --> 00:08:12.524
Pages is what we want.

00:08:12.524 --> 00:08:14.917
So we go to pages.

00:08:14.917 --> 00:08:18.497
And GitHub Pages is designed to host
personal organization or project pages

00:08:18.497 --> 00:08:20.257
on the GitHub repository.

00:08:20.257 --> 00:08:24.899
So there's only one thing we really
need to do here.

00:08:24.899 --> 00:08:27.359
Git repositories have different branches.

00:08:27.359 --> 00:08:28.819
There will be like a main branch.

00:08:28.819 --> 00:08:30.449
There could also be an
experimental branch.

00:08:30.449 --> 00:08:33.840
If you're like kind of going back and
forth between like something you're

00:08:33.840 --> 00:08:36.543
working on and something that's public.

00:08:36.543 --> 00:08:43.205
At this stage, if you're your process as
an emerging digital media producer, as a

00:08:43.205 --> 00:08:47.569
emerging creative coder, you don't
really need to worry about that yet.

00:08:47.569 --> 00:08:51.749
It's kind of more advanced stuff,
especially as you start working on

00:08:51.749 --> 00:08:56.574
projects with multiple people someday.

00:08:56.574 --> 00:08:58.345
And so we only have one
branch in our project.

00:08:58.345 --> 00:09:00.368
It's called main.

00:09:00.368 --> 00:09:03.989
But even though there's only one branch,
that's not what's selected by

00:09:03.989 --> 00:09:05.218
default in GitHub Pages.

00:09:05.218 --> 00:09:07.021
So we have to hit main.

00:09:07.021 --> 00:09:09.767
We leave this at root.

00:09:09.767 --> 00:09:13.467
If we wanted to set a different directory
in our repository to host the pages

00:09:13.467 --> 00:09:15.748
from, we could do that.

00:09:15.748 --> 00:09:16.548
But we don't need to.

00:09:16.548 --> 00:09:21.696
And I hit save.

00:09:21.696 --> 00:09:26.417
So it's building the web page out of the
files that I've uploaded.

00:09:26.417 --> 00:09:29.242
Right now, it's just an image.

00:09:29.242 --> 00:09:34.388
So sometimes that takes a
little bit of time.

00:09:34.388 --> 00:09:35.568
This is a free service.

00:09:35.568 --> 00:09:40.085
It hasn't finished yet.

00:09:40.085 --> 00:09:41.765
It's currently being built
from the main branch.

00:09:41.765 --> 00:09:43.036
See that?

00:09:43.036 --> 00:09:47.655
I'm going to hit reload again.

00:09:47.655 --> 00:09:54.275
The URL will be a predictable
one, which is nice.

00:09:54.275 --> 00:09:56.316
So just any minute now.

00:09:56.316 --> 00:09:57.796
Sometimes you get what you
pay for it, right?

00:09:57.796 --> 00:10:01.906
Sometimes it'll take a whole minute,
which in between saving something and

00:10:01.906 --> 00:10:03.926
wanting to test it can feel
like a long time.

00:10:03.926 --> 00:10:06.261
So here we go.

00:10:06.261 --> 00:10:07.762
And it's saying page not found.

00:10:07.762 --> 00:10:13.907
But if I go to rosie_a.png,
here's Rosie.

00:10:13.907 --> 00:10:14.747
That's great.

00:10:14.747 --> 00:10:17.772
Hi, Rosie.

00:10:17.772 --> 00:10:20.123
Why did it say page not found?

00:10:20.123 --> 00:10:29.648
So web servers generally will look for a
file underneath that last slash, right?

00:10:29.648 --> 00:10:32.088
And this URL up here, it's HTTPS.

00:10:32.088 --> 00:10:38.004
That's a secure hypertext
transfer protocol.

00:10:38.004 --> 00:10:43.366
And this is my username
on GitHub. - github.io.

00:10:43.366 --> 00:10:44.286
It's not dot com.

00:10:44.286 --> 00:10:46.954
It's dot io.

00:10:46.954 --> 00:10:47.954
And then it's slash.

00:10:47.954 --> 00:10:51.772
And it's the repository name with the
dash in it, which I didn't really want.

00:10:51.772 --> 00:10:54.929
But there it is.

00:10:54.929 --> 00:10:59.649
If we just wanted to have something load
at this address without designating a

00:10:59.649 --> 00:11:03.241
file name, then we need
a file called index.html.

00:11:03.241 --> 00:11:06.813
So if I go to code here, we
have our one file.

00:11:06.813 --> 00:11:07.973
Well, we want another file.

00:11:07.973 --> 00:11:10.724
We're going to create a new file.

00:11:10.724 --> 00:11:13.396
I'm going to call this index.html.

00:11:13.396 --> 00:11:23.878
And I'm just going to make a really bare
bones piece of HTML.

00:11:23.878 --> 00:11:28.369
We'll have HTML.

00:11:28.369 --> 00:11:29.600
We will have a head.

00:11:29.600 --> 00:11:31.100
You can see it has some
syntax highlighting.

00:11:31.100 --> 00:11:32.292
That's nice.

00:11:32.292 --> 00:11:36.268
Title.

00:11:36.268 --> 00:11:39.988
We'll have a body.

00:11:44.121 --> 00:11:47.693
And I'm using the image tag, which if you
haven't already learned

00:11:47.693 --> 00:11:49.354
about, you're about to.

00:11:49.354 --> 00:11:51.767
This will put an image on the page.

00:11:51.767 --> 00:11:54.878
And it's loading from the
same directory we're in.

00:11:54.878 --> 00:11:56.518
So I don't need to do any pathing.

00:11:56.518 --> 00:12:00.071
And I don't any HTTP.

00:12:00.071 --> 00:12:04.726
So I am going to commit my changes.

00:12:04.726 --> 00:12:07.226
I'm going to say, yeah,
create index.html.

00:12:07.226 --> 00:12:07.766
That's great.

00:12:07.766 --> 00:12:08.566
That's descriptive.

00:12:08.566 --> 00:12:10.989
That's actually what I'm doing.

00:12:10.989 --> 00:12:17.507
And automatically, in about a minute, our
page not found is going to change.

00:12:17.507 --> 00:12:26.342
So when you're working in CodePen, you're
going to be on a different site

00:12:26.342 --> 00:12:28.037
than the GitHub asset.

00:12:28.037 --> 00:12:32.657
And so what is the URL going to be for
the image that's over here?

00:12:32.657 --> 00:12:35.814
Well, we'll look at that in a second.

00:12:35.814 --> 00:12:38.014
So we can see that there's a URL here.

00:12:38.014 --> 00:12:44.902
If I open this in a new tab, we're going
to get this embedded in a viewer here.

00:12:44.902 --> 00:12:48.542
There's an alpha channel on this PNG,
which is why the black background is

00:12:48.542 --> 00:12:51.504
showing through, as opposed
to the white box.

00:12:51.504 --> 00:12:56.254
And if we open the image in a new tab,
then we get this long URL here of

00:12:56.254 --> 00:13:00.674
raw.github user content with my username
and the repository name, and then

00:13:00.674 --> 00:13:02.074
refs, heads, main, rosie.

00:13:02.074 --> 00:13:03.854
That's a lot.

00:13:03.854 --> 00:13:06.974
Even if you're copying and pasting, copy
and paste this into code

00:13:06.974 --> 00:13:09.277
pen, it probably works.

00:13:09.277 --> 00:13:13.640
But better than that, if I reload this,
see, now our HTML is loading.

00:13:13.640 --> 00:13:19.534
The index.html does not appear in the
URL, but it is implicitly being loaded.

00:13:19.534 --> 00:13:26.469
So in the same way, rosy.underscorea.png,
it's like the URL is predictable.

00:13:26.469 --> 00:13:34.381
It's aphid.github.io, or your
username.githube.io, slash the

00:13:34.381 --> 00:13:38.713
repository name, slash whatever
the file name is.

00:13:38.713 --> 00:13:44.386
And if you remember that pattern, yes,
you can copy and paste from here, but

00:13:44.386 --> 00:13:48.307
you can also kind of freehand it,
because you know how it's going to be.

00:13:48.307 --> 00:13:54.358
https colon, my username.github.io, slash
ftm, whatever you called your

00:13:54.358 --> 00:13:59.083
repository, slash whatever
the file name is.

00:13:59.083 --> 00:14:04.826
And you can embed sounds, you can embed
other CSS files and things, and you'll

00:14:04.826 --> 00:14:06.298
be learning how to do all of that.

00:14:06.298 --> 00:14:10.490
But I just wanted to get you to the point
where you can upload some images and

00:14:10.490 --> 00:14:12.562
access them on another page.

00:14:12.562 --> 00:14:16.659
So yeah.

00:14:16.659 --> 00:14:21.662
Hopefully that was clear, but I will be
hanging out in the chat

00:14:21.662 --> 00:14:23.543
room for the class.

00:14:23.543 --> 00:14:25.755
I will be looking at my email.

00:14:25.755 --> 00:14:30.596
And if you need help, please ask, because
this is something that we'll

00:14:30.596 --> 00:14:32.478
be using all quarter.

00:14:32.478 --> 00:14:38.838
And so let's get it right in week ones,
so that the rest of the quarter, you can

00:14:38.838 --> 00:14:41.202
focus on the code.

00:14:41.202 --> 00:14:42.902
Well, thanks for sticking around
for all of that.

00:14:42.902 --> 00:14:47.643
And I look forward to working with you
and seeing what you make.

00:14:47.643 --> 00:14:50.161
So have a good one and take care.

